<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
   Auto Backup on Android 11 and lower (android:fullBackupContent). Android 12 and higher read
   data_extraction_rules.xml instead; the two lists must stay the same, and BackupRulesTest pins
   that they do.

   Excluded, because each holds something that works as a credential on its own:
   - plainticker.xml: the shared preferences file DevicePassStore keeps this device's Pro code in,
     sent raw in X-PT-Code. A backup restored elsewhere would be a second device holding the same
     bearer code. The watchlist, the onboarding flag and the last digest share this file, and a
     rule can only exclude a whole file, so they are not restored either: all three are cheap to
     rebuild, and moving the code to a file of its own would mean migrating a paid credential
     whose loss orphans the pass it is bound to. The same file keeps the 26-symbol code a legacy
     device has minted but the server has not yet confirmed (device_pass_pending_new_code, a
     credential too), the note You shows after a rekey, and a sign-out still waiting for
     the server; BackupRulesTest pins that DevicePassStore writes every one of its keys here.
     Since 2026-09-27 both codes are sealed at rest (device_pass_code_sealed,
     device_pass_pending_new_code_sealed) with a Keystore key that never leaves the phone; the
     sealed keys live in this same file, so they are excluded with it. Since 1.3.28 the app lock's
     setting (app_lock_enabled) lives here too, like the other security preferences: a lock
     restored onto another phone, or onto this one after its screen lock changed, would be a lock
     nobody chose there.
   - datastore/account.preferences_pb: the signed-in Google account, which only means anything
     next to the device code above.
   - wallet_session.bin (and its .tmp): the saved MWA session. It is sealed with an Android
     Keystore key that never leaves this phone, so a copy could not be opened anyway; it is
     excluded so no copy exists.

   Everything else is backed up: the swap, vote and pass receipts are the app's own record of
   public on-chain transactions, and nothing in them can act for anyone.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="plainticker.xml" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/account.preferences_pb" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="wallet_session.bin" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="wallet_session.bin.tmp" />
</full-backup-content>
